Evaluation
Your instinct on folder-per-table is correct and I'd keep it — it's a vertical slice, and it means a change request about Accounts touches exactly one directory. Your instinct on one class per operation is also correct. The problem is the middle layer: the per-table abstract base class that implements IPlugin.

What's working
Element	Verdict
Folder per table	Keep. Scales to 200 tables without becoming a swamp.
One class per operation	Keep, but sharpen it — one class per registered step, not per operation. Update often needs both a pre-op and a post-op step, and the class name should tell you which.
Utils for common helpers	Keep the idea, split the class. A single CommonHelpers becomes a 3,000-line dumping ground by month six.
What will hurt you at enterprise scale
1. The per-table base class is the main flaw. Four concrete failures:

Fragile base class. AccountPluginBase accumulates every helper any step ever needed. After two years it's 40 protected methods, and every step inherits all 40 whether it uses one or thirty. Changing a signature forces re-review of every unrelated step on that table.
You spend your single inheritance slot on the wrong thing. Because the table base implements IPlugin, your genuine cross-cutting concerns — tracing, exception shaping, depth guards, context construction — have to be duplicated per table base or pushed into a second base layer, producing a three-deep hierarchy.
Pre and post have genuinely different shapes. Pre-op mutates the Target; post-op reads images and writes elsewhere. A shared parent invites helpers that silently behave differently by stage — exactly where stage-dependent bugs live.
Testability. You can't substitute one shared method; you must satisfy the whole base class surface.
The fix is composition: one PluginBase for infrastructure only, and table behaviour in small focused Services that handlers construct.

2. A "Dataverse Web API operations" class inside a plugin is an architectural defect. Inside the sandbox you already have IOrganizationService injected. Going out over HTTP leaves the transaction (your writes no longer roll back with the operation), burns your 2-minute budget on network latency, and requires the sandbox to hold credentials it shouldn't have. For genuine external calls, use an async step, a Service Endpoint, or Power Automate.

3. Missing entirely from the template: business rules separated from SDK types (so they're testable in milliseconds), a repository boundary for queries, registration metadata living next to the code, recursion control beyond Depth, secrets handling via environment variables, unit tests, signing, and ALM.

